# Add only confirmed external-library races, with the finding documented here.
# Arch's uninstrumented Qt 6.12 posts QFutureCallOutEvents on pooled threads
# and deletes them in QCoreApplication::removePostedEvents during watcher
# teardown. The event-queue mutex lives in Qt, so TSan misses that ordering.
# Match only this event reclamation frame, not QtConcurrent worker frames.
race:QCoreApplication::removePostedEvents
# Arch's Qt is not built with TSan, so the atomic reference count that orders
# a QFuture's final release is invisible to it. Whichever side drops the last
# reference frees the shared state (result store, wait condition) after the
# worker is done with it; TSan sees only the unordered accesses. Seen when a
# ViewerSession is destroyed right after its listing finished. Every report
# runs through the QFuture destructor, so match that frame only.
race:::~QFuture()
# Qt's internal allocations (thread pool bookkeeping, container growth inside
# libQt6Core) are guarded by Qt's own mutexes, which the uninstrumented library
# never reports. Seen as a malloc in one pool thread and a realloc in another,
# with no Omaroll frame in either access. Omaroll never calls realloc itself;
# its own container accesses are instrumented and reported where they happen.
# TSan matches the interceptor's internal name (___interceptor_realloc), so
# anchor only the end; every C++ frame ends in its parameter list instead.
race:realloc$
# glibc caches strings such as the time zone name with strdup and frees the old
# copy later, under its own internal lock (Qt calls tzset() while converting
# file times on worker threads). Seen as a strdup on a pool thread and a free
# inside libc on the main thread. Omaroll never calls strdup.
race:strdup$
# Qt D-Bus runs its own QDBusConnection thread. libQt6DBus allocates events on
# the calling thread and Qt deletes them on that thread after delivery, through
# the uninstrumented event queue. No Omaroll code runs on the D-Bus thread, so
# match any report with a libQt6DBus frame.
race:libQt6DBus.so
# A queued invokeMethod allocates its call event inside Qt on the posting
# thread, and Qt deletes it on the receiving thread after delivery, through
# the uninstrumented event queue. Match the allocating Qt frame, never
# sendPostedEvents, which every queued slot runs under.
race:QMetaObject::invokeMethodImpl
